Let’s break the difference between **ALB** and **NLB** in a simple, clear way.

---

## 🔹 Application Load Balancer (ALB)

Application Load Balancer

**Works at:** **Layer 7 (Application layer)**
**Best for:** Web applications (HTTP / HTTPS)

### Key Features

* Supports **HTTP & HTTPS**
* **Path-based routing** (`/login`, `/api`, `/images`)
* **Host-based routing** (`app.example.com`, `api.example.com`)
* Supports **WebSockets**
* Integrates with **AWS WAF**
* Returns **HTTP response codes** (404, 503, etc.)

### Example

> Route `/api` traffic to backend servers and `/images` to another set of servers.

✅ Ideal for **microservices**, **REST APIs**, **web apps**

---

## 🔹 Network Load Balancer (NLB)

Network Load Balancer

**Works at:** **Layer 4 (Transport layer)**
**Best for:** High-performance & low-latency workloads

### Key Features

* Supports **TCP, UDP, TLS**
* Handles **millions of requests per second**
* **Ultra-low latency**
* Preserves **client IP address**
* Static IP support
* More fault-tolerant than ALB

### Example

> Load balancing for a **database**, **gaming server**, or **real-time streaming app**

✅ Ideal for **non-HTTP traffic** and **extreme performance needs**

---

## 🔑 ALB vs NLB (Quick Comparison)

| Feature     | ALB               | NLB                          |
| ----------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------- |
| OSI Layer   | Layer 7           | Layer 4                      |
| Protocols   | HTTP / HTTPS      | TCP / UDP / TLS              |
| Routing     | Path & Host based | No routing logic             |
| Performance | Moderate          | Extremely high               |
| Latency     | Slightly higher   | Ultra-low                    |
| Client IP   | ❌ Not preserved   | ✅ Preserved                  |
| Use Case    | Web apps, APIs    | Databases, gaming, streaming |

---

## 🧠 Easy Interview Tip

* **ALB = Smart routing for web traffic**
* **NLB = Super-fast traffic forwarding**
